Caches are fundamental to modern processor performance, but are also a well-known source of side-channel vulnerabilities. Recent advancements in “weird computation” leverage data caches in conjunction with speculative execution to enable hidden computations, primarily for program obfuscation. This work extends the paradigm of microarchitectural weird machines to the TLB, an address translation cache that has received less attention in this context and is often less susceptible to common cache defense techniques. We demonstrate the feasibility of using TLB entries as “weird registers” to encode binary states and construct logic gates. Our adaptation of the Flexo compiler framework, termed Flexo-TLB, enables the creation of complex weird circuits on the TLB. Through extensive experimentation on modern Intel Meteorlake P-core and E-core and Kabylake architectures, we characterize the timing behavior of the TLB, optimize transient window sizes, and evaluate the performance and accuracy of TLB-based weird gates and circuits. While proving the viability of the TLB as an alternative microarchitectural state for hidden computations, we identify key limitations, including a reduced gate input capacity and increased sensitivity to circuit complexity and architectural differences. Our findings highlight the potential of the TLB as a resilient, albeit constrained, medium for microarchitectural computation, particularly in scenarios where conventional data cache-based approaches are mitigated.